tion due to a fist fight</article-title><source>Int J STD AIDS</source><year>2008</year><volume>19</volume><fpage>131</fpage><lpage>132</lpage><pub-id pub-id-type="pmid">18334072</pub-id><pub-id pub-id-type="doi">10.1258/ijsa.2007.007156</pub-id></citation></ref><ref id="B8"><citation citation-type="journal"><person-group person-group-type="author"><name><surname>O'Farrell</surname><given-names>N</given-names></name><name><surname>Tovey</surname><given-names>SJ</given-names></name><name><surname>Morgan-Capner</surname><given-names>P</given-names></name></person-group><article-title>Transmission of HIV-1 infection after a fight</article-title><source>Lancet</source><year>1992</year><volume>339</volume><fpage>246</fpage><pub-id pub-id-type="pmid">1346200</pub-id><pub-id pub-id-type="doi">10.1016/0140-6736(92)90048-8</pub-id></citation></ref><ref id="B9"><citation citation-type="journal"><person-group person-group-type="author"><name><surname>Singer</surname><given-names>M</given-names></name><name><surname>Simmons</surname><given-names>J</given-names></name><name><surname>Duke</surname><given-names>M</given-names></name><name><surname>Broomhall</surname><given-names>L</given-names></name></person-group><article-title>The challenges of street research on drug use, violence, and AIDS risk</article-title><source>Addiction Research &#x00026; Theory</source><year>2001</year><volume>9</volume><fpage>365</fpage><lpage>404</lpage><pub-id pub-id-type="doi">10.3109/16066350109141759</pub-id></citation></ref><ref id="B10"><citation citation-type="book"><person-group person-group-type="author"><name><surname>Fagan</surname><given-names>J</given-names></name><name><surname>Chin</surname><given-names>K</given-names></name></person-group><person-group person-group-type="editor"><name><surname>De La Rosa M, Lambert EY and Gropper B</surname></name></person-group><article-title>Violence as regulation and social control in the distribution of crack</article-title><source>Drugs and Violence: Causes, Correlates, and Consequences</source><year>1990</year><publisher-name>, NIDA</publisher-name><fpage>8</fpage><lpage>42</lpage></citation></ref><ref id="B11"><citation citation-type="book"><person-group person-group-type="author"><name><surname>Maher</surname><given-names>L</given-names></name></person-group><source>Sexed Work: Gender, Race, and Resistance in a Brooklyn Drug Market.</source><year> 2000</year><publisher-name>, Oxford University Press</publisher-name></citation></ref><ref id="B12"><citation citation-type="book"><person-group person-group-type="author"><name><surname>Sterk</surname><given-names>CE</given-names></name><name><surname>Elifson</surname><given-names>K</given-names></name></person-group><person-group person-group-type="editor"><name><surname>De La Rosa M LEYGB</surname></name></person-group><article-title>Drug-related violence and street prostitution</article-title><source>Drugs and Violence: Causes, Correlates, and Consequences</source><year>1990</year><fpage>208</fpage><lpage>220</lpage></citation></ref><ref id="B13"><citation citation-type="journal"><person-group person-group-type="author"><name><surname>Parker</surname><given-names>RN</given-names></name><name><surname>Auerhahn</surname><given-names>K</given-names></name></person-group><article-title>Alcohol, drugs, and violence</article-title><source>Annu Rev Sociol</source><year>1998</year><volume>24</volume><fpage>291</fpage><lpage>311</lpage><pub-id pub-id-type="doi">10.1146/annurev.soc.24.1.291</pub-id></citation></ref><ref id="B14"><citation citation-type="book"><person-group person-group-type="author"><name><surname>Erickson</surname><given-names>PG</given-names></name></person-group><source>Drugs, Violence and Public Health: What does the harm reduction approach have to offer.</source><year>2001</year><publisher-name>, Fraser Institute</publisher-name></citation></ref></ref-list></back></article>